/math></p><p>as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>></mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></math></p>","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2019","_marksId":"64c0e80e6f5bfc7ab78aea31"},{"id":18344,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Inverse Trigonometric Functions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∞</mo></munderover><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math> is equal to","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>π</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>","None of these"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>Given, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∞</mo></munderover><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></mfenced></math></p><p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><msup><mfenced><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfenced><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mi>ii</mi></mfenced></math></p><p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math> </p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><mrow><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mi>iii</mi></mfenced></math></p><p>On putting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>…</mo></math> successively in Eq. (iii), we get</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mn>7</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mn>3</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mn>13</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mn>7</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>On adding vertically, we get</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∞</mo></munderover><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>∞</mo></mrow></munder><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∞</mo></munderover><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math>   [from Eq. (i)]</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>∞</mo></mrow></munder><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2018","_marksId":"64c0e7fd6f5bfc7ab78ae99c"},{"id":18345,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Inverse Trigonometric Functions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> and<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>·</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>∀</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></math> is equal to","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∵</mo><mo> </mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>Given that, </p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>Which is possible only when</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo> </mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p>Put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p>Then, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math></p><p>and put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p>then, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo> </mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>·</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p>","difficulty":"Hard","source":"BITSAT 2017","_marksId":"64c0e7f26f5bfc7ab78ae93c"},{"id":18346,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Inverse Trigonometric Functions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"If $\\sin ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2 a}{1+a^{2}}\\right)-\\cos ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1-b^{2}}{1+b^{2}}\\right)=\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2 x}{1-x^{2}}\\right)$,\r\n\r\nthen what is the value of $x$ ?","options":["$a / b$","$a b$","$b / a$","$\\frac{a-b}{1+a b}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"Given,\r\n\r\n$\\sin ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2 a}{1+a^{2}}\\right)-\\cos ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1-b^{2}}{1+b^{2}}\\right)=\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2 x}{1-x^{2}}\\right)\\therefore \\quad 2 \\tan ^{-1} a-2 \\tan ^{-1} b=2 \\tan ^{-1} x\\Rightarrow \\tan ^{-1} a-\\tan ^{-1} b=\\tan ^{-1} x\\Rightarrow \\quad \\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{a-b}{1+a b}\\right)=\\tan ^{-1} x\\Rightarrow \\quad x=\\frac{a-b}{1+a b}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2016","_marksId":"64c0e7e06f5bfc7ab78ae89f"},{"id":18347,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Inverse Trigonometric Functions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"If $\\cos ^{-1} x-\\cos ^{-1} \\frac{y}{2}=\\alpha$,\r\n\r\nthen $4 x^{2}-4 x y \\cos \\alpha+y^{2}$ is equal to","options":["$2 \\sin 2 \\alpha$","4","$4 \\sin ^{2} \\alpha$","$-4 \\sin ^{2} \\alpha$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\cos ^{-1} x-\\cos ^{-1} \\frac{y}{2}=\\alpha\\Rightarrow \\cos ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{x y}{2}+\\sqrt{\\left(1-x^{2}\\right)\\left(1-\\frac{y^{2}}{4}\\right)}\\right)=\\alpha\\Rightarrow \\cos ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{x y+\\sqrt{4-y^{2}-4 x^{2}+x^{2} y^{2}}}{2}\\right)=\\alpha\\Rightarrow 4 x^{2}+y^{2}-4 x y \\cos \\alpha=4 \\sin ^{2} \\alpha$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2015","_marksId":"64c0e7d26f5bfc7ab78ae826"},{"id":18348,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Inverse Trigonometric Functions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Find the value of $\\tan \\left[2 \\tan ^{-1} \\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right]$","options":["$-1 / 3$","$-7 / 17$","$-1 / 2$","$-1 / 4$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} & 2 \\tan ^{-1} \\frac{1}{5}=\\tan ^{-1} \\frac{1}{5}+\\tan ^{-1} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ &=\\tan ^{-1} \\frac{\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}}{1-\\frac{1}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}}=\\tan ^{-1} \\frac{2 / 5}{24 / 25}=\\tan ^{-1} \\frac{5}{12} \\\\ &=\\tan \\left(2 \\tan ^{-1} \\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\tan \\left(\\tan ^{-1} \\frac{5}{12}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\tan \\left(\\tan ^{-1} \\frac{5}{12}\\right)-\\tan \\frac{\\pi}{4}}{1+\\tan \\left(\\tan ^{-1} \\frac{5}{12}\\right) \\tan \\frac{\\pi}{4}}=\\frac{-\\frac{7}{12}}{\\frac{17}{12}}=-\\frac{7}{17} \\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2014","_marksId":"64c0e7c46f5bfc7ab78ae7b6"},{"id":18349,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Inverse Trigonometric Functions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"The greatest and least values of $\\left(\\sin ^{-1} x\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\cos ^{-1} x\\right)^{2}$ are respectively","options":["$\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{4}$ and 0","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\frac{-\\pi}{2}$","$\\frac{5 \\pi^{2}}{4}$ and $\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{8}$","$\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{4}$ and $\\frac{-\\pi^{2}}{4}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"We have, $\\left(\\sin ^{-1} x\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\cos ^{-1} x\\right)^{2}=\\left(\\sin ^{-1} x+\\cos ^{-1} x\\right)^{2}-2 \\sin ^{-1} x \\cdot \\cos ^{-1} x=\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{4}-2 \\sin ^{-1} x\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\sin ^{-1} x\\right)=\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{4}-\\pi \\sin ^{-1} x+2\\left(\\sin ^{-1} x\\right)^{2}=2\\left[\\left(\\sin ^{-1} x\\right)^{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\sin ^{-1} x+\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{8}\\right]=2\\left[\\left(\\sin ^{-1} x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)^{2}+\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{16}\\right]$\r\n\r\nThus, the least value is $2\\left(\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{16}\\right)$ i.e. $\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{8}$\r\n\r\nand the greatest value is $2\\left[\\left(\\frac{-\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)^{2}+\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{16}\\right]$ i.e. $\\frac{5 \\pi^{2}}{4} .$","difficulty":"Hard","source":"BITSAT 2013","_marksId":"64c0e7b66f5bfc7ab78ae738"},{"id":18350,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Inverse Trigonometric Functions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"The value of\r\n\r\n$\\cos \\left[\\frac{1}{2} \\cos ^{-1}\\left(\\cos \\left(\\sin ^{-1} \\frac{\\sqrt{63}}{8}\\right)\\right)\\right]$ is $-$","options":["$3 / 16$","$3 / 8$","$3 / 4$","$3 / 2$"],"answer":2,"solution":"The given trigonometric ratio\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\\begin{array}{l}\r\n\r\n=\\cos \\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos \\left(\\cos ^{-1} \\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\right)\\right] \\\\\r\n\r\n=\\cos \\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\cos ^{-1} \\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\\r\n\r\n=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\cos \\left(\\cos ^{-1} \\frac{1}{8}\\right)}{2}}=\\frac{3}{4}\r\n\r\n\\end{array}\r\n\r\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2013","_marksId":"64c0e7b66f5bfc7ab78ae739"},{"id":18351,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Inverse Trigonometric Functions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"$\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)$ equal to-","options":["$\\frac{1}{2} \\cos ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)$","$\\frac{1}{2} \\sin ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)$","$\\frac{1}{2} \\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)$","$\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)\\quad=\\tan ^{-1}\\left[\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{9}}{1-\\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{2}{9}}\\right]=\\tan ^{-1}\\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\right]$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2012","_marksId":"64c0e7a66f5bfc7ab78ae6b6"},{"id":18352,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Inverse Trigonometric Functions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"If $k \\leq \\sin ^{-1} x+\\cos ^{-1} x+\\tan ^{-1} x \\leq K,$ then $-$","options":["$\\mathrm{k}=0, \\mathrm{~K}=\\pi$","$\\mathrm{k}=0, \\mathrm{~K}=\\pi / 2$","$\\mathrm{k}=\\pi / 2, \\mathrm{~K}=\\pi$","None of these"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\sin ^{-1} x+\\cos ^{-1} x+\\tan ^{-1} x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+\\tan ^{-1} x$\r\nSince domain of the function $\\mathrm{x} \\in[-1,1]\\therefore-\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\tan ^{-1} x \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{4}$\r\nHence, $\\mathrm{k}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\mathrm{K}=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$","difficulty":"Hard","source":"BITSAT 2012","_marksId":"64c0e7a76f5bfc7ab78ae6b7"},{"id":18353,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Inverse Trigonometric Functions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"The value of \\(\\sin \\left(4 \\tan ^{-1} \\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\cos \\left(2 \\tan ^{-1} \\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\) is","options":["\\(3 / 7\\)","\\(7 / 8\\)","\\(8 / 21\\)","None of these"],"answer":3,"solution":"\\(\\sin \\left(4 \\tan ^{-1} \\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\)\n\\(\\begin{aligned}\n&= 2 \\sin \\left(2 \\tan ^{-1} \\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\cos \\left(2 \\tan ^{-1} \\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&=2 \\sin \\left(\\tan ^{-1} \\frac{3}{4}\\right)=\\cos \\left(\\tan ^{-1} \\frac{3}{4}\\right)=2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{24}{25} \\\\\n& \\cos \\left(2 \\tan ^{-1} \\frac{1}{7}\\right)=\\cos \\left(\\tan ^{-1} \\frac{7}{24}\\right)=\\frac{24}{25} .\n\\end{aligned}\\)\nThe given expression \\(=0\\)","difficulty":"Hard","source":"BITSAT 2011","_marksId":"64c0e7996f5bfc7ab78ae641"}]}